  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 235 AM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry, seasonable weather for Halloween weekend \ncontinues into early next week. Clouds will increase on Sunday and \nMonday as a shortwave approaches and crosses the area, but no rain \nis expected. \n\nA cold front is well to our south early this morning, with NNE winds \ngusting to 20-25 mph near the coast in the wake of the front (and \nlighter winds inland). CAA is not particularly strong for the last \nday of October, but temps have fallen into the upper 40s-lower 60s \nand will fall to the mid 40s-lower 50s by sunrise.  Cooler, dry, and \npleasant today, with highs in the lower to middle 60s. High pressure \nbuilds over the area this evening through Sunday, which will allow \nfor winds to diminish. Given the cool, dry airmass and diminishing \nwinds, temps fall off quickly (to the upper 40s-50s) during the \nevening. Certainly no weather issues for those trick-or-treating. \nLows Sunday morning drop into the upper 30s-mid 40s. A shortwave \napproaches on Sunday and crosses the area on Monday, which will \nallow for cloud cover to increase. However, due to the lack of low-\nlevel moisture, rain is not expected. It will turn slightly milder \non Sunday and Monday, with a few 70F readings possible Monday \nafternoon across southern VA/NE NC. Cool again Monday morning with \nupper 30s-mid 40s in most areas. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain chances may return by Thursday with above \naverage temperatures expected for the first few days of November.\n\nThe gradual warming trend continues into mid next week, as an upper \nridge builds over the eastern CONUS while surface high pressure \nmoves well offshore. Widespread lower-mid 70s appear likely on both \nTuesday and Wednesday. A low pressure system is progged to develop \nin the Plains on Tuesday before approaching the Mid-Atlantic by late \nnext week. Precipitation chances finally return to the forecast by \nnext Thursday-Thursday night and continue into Friday. However, \nthere are lots of different solutions with respect to the track, \nstrength, and timing of the system. So while some of the models do \nshow widespread rainfall, others do not. Therefore, forecast \nconfidence in this potential welcome rainfall remains understandably \nlow. Above average temperatures likely continue through the end of \nnext week, with no potential freezes in the forecast as the more \nsubstantial cold air remains in the northern half of Canada for the \nforeseeable future.",
